## 1A. Authoritative target architecture

This runbook deploys the complete ShiftWeave application, not an MCP-only process:

```text
Public HTTPS request
    -> Elastic IP / DNS
    -> Caddy on Account A EC2
    -> ShiftWeave Express process
    -> React dashboard, REST APIs, or public /mcp
    -> validated workflow services
    -> SQLite on the attached EBS volume
```

The internal simulator path is deliberately separate from the public path:

```text
Alexa+-style simulator agent
    -> MCPClientBridge
    -> http://127.0.0.1:<port>/mcp
    -> the same session-isolated MCP server implementation
```

The loopback address means the bridge does not traverse the public DNS name, Elastic IP, Caddy, or the internet. It is still an HTTP MCP request, but it is host-local. Do not expose the bridge listener on `0.0.0.0`; only the public Express `/mcp` listener is externally reachable through Caddy.

The agent/model boundary is:

```text
Simulator agent
    -> STS AssumeRole
    -> Account B Bedrock runtime
    -> model planning response
    -> MCPClientBridge
    -> deterministic MCP tool call
    -> ShiftWeave workflow service
```

MCP tool handlers do not call Bedrock. They validate arguments, enforce workflow and clinical boundaries, perform the requested deterministic operation, persist idempotency/checkpoint/audit state, and return structured results. The model may plan a tool call, but it cannot authorize custody transfer, resolve a conflict, administer medication, change a clinical order, or publish a note without the existing deterministic guards and human review.

### Account ownership

| Resource | Account | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| EC2, Elastic IP, EBS, ECR | Account A | Host and application runtime |
| SSM Parameter Store | Account A | Runtime configuration and protected values |
| SSM Session Manager | Account A | SSH-less host administration |
| CloudWatch and EBS snapshots | Account A | Runtime observability and storage recovery |
| S3 backup bucket | Account A | Off-host SQLite-consistent backups |
| Account A budget | Account A | Host and infrastructure spend guardrail |
| Cross-account Bedrock role | Account B | Narrow trust boundary for model invocation |
| Bedrock runtime and model usage | Account B | Model calls and AI spend |
| Account B budget | Account B | Bedrock spend guardrail |

This is a single-instance, stateful architecture. It is not multi-AZ, horizontally scalable, or zero-downtime. SQLite remains attached to one EC2 instance, and active MCP sessions remain process-local. Durable workflow checkpoints survive restarts; an HTTP MCP session may need to be re-established after a restart.

### Why the internal bridge is local

The simulator agent and MCP server run in the same ShiftWeave deployment. Calling `127.0.0.1`:

- avoids an unnecessary public network hop;
- avoids DNS, TLS termination, and Internet Gateway traversal for internal traffic;
- keeps the public MCP surface separate from the internal agent loop;
- preserves the same MCP discovery and tool contracts;
- does not imply that the model is embedded in the MCP server.

The loopback path is not a substitute for authentication on the public endpoint. External clients still use HTTPS and the configured MCP bearer token.

### Why this does not automatically reduce TTFT

The current application buffers model responses and does not expose true token streaming to the browser. Its timing events represent complete model-planning, tool, and voice-turn durations, not browser-visible first-token latency.

The architecture can reduce avoidable local overhead by keeping the MCP bridge on loopback and placing EC2 and Bedrock in the same region, but the dominant latency is normally the Bedrock request, model selection, model queueing, prompt/tool-schema size, and number of planning iterations. Do not claim a TTFT improvement from `t4g.medium` without a controlled benchmark.

Measure these separately:

```text
model request start -> first model event received
model request start -> complete model response
MCP tool start       -> tool result
voice turn start     -> final rendered response
```

Report warm/cold runs, successful/failed calls, sample counts, p50 and p95. If true TTFT is required, implement and validate provider streaming and cancellation-aware UI behavior separately; Streamable HTTP alone does not prove token streaming.

