## 9. Build the Docker image

The existing `Dockerfile` builds:

- Backend TypeScript
- React frontend
- Production Node dependencies
- Non-root runtime image
- Readiness health check

Because the target instance is ARM64, build and publish an ARM64 image. A normal Docker build on an x86 Windows machine may produce `linux/amd64`, which cannot be assumed to run on `t4g.medium`.

Create or select a Buildx builder:

```powershell
docker buildx create --name shiftweave-builder --use
docker buildx inspect --bootstrap
```

Build and test the target image locally:

```powershell
docker buildx build `
  --platform linux/arm64 `
  -t shiftweave:initial `
  --load `
  .
```

Inspect the local image:

```powershell
docker image inspect shiftweave:initial `
  --format "{{.Os}}/{{.Architecture}}"
```

The result must be:

```text
linux/arm64
```

The native `better-sqlite3` dependency is the critical compatibility check. The source code is portable, but the image must contain a Linux ARM64-compatible native module. Run the image and verify `/readyz`, SQLite initialization, and one database query before pushing it.

If you also need an x86 image, publish a multi-architecture manifest:

```powershell
docker buildx build `
  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 `
  -t ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/shiftweave:TAG `
  --push `
  .
```

The image contains the application code, but it must not contain:

- Production `.env`
- Epic private keys
- AWS access keys
- MCP tokens
- Production database files

