## 23. Configure Bedrock optionally

Start with mock mode:

```env
LLM_PROVIDER=mock
```

This keeps the deployment free from model calls and simplifies initial verification.

When ready to test Bedrock:

```env
LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock
AWS_REGION=us-west-2
BEDROCK_MODEL_ID=approved-model-id
BEDROCK_ROLE_ARN=arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:role/ShiftWeaveBedrockInvokeRole
```

The simulator agent assumes `BEDROCK_ROLE_ARN` from the Account A EC2 role and uses the temporary credentials for the Account B Bedrock runtime client. Verify the role ARN, external ID/trust conditions, target Region, and model access before enabling the provider.

Do not put these in the server environment if an instance role is available:

```env
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
```

Bedrock remains optional. The application should still run in mock mode.

For interactive use, prefer the Bedrock Converse streaming API through the SDK when the application is ready to expose partial output. The current runbook does not claim true TTFT because the current application buffers model responses. Always set an explicit maximum output token count and use the repository's provider error handling; do not add hidden fallback text when a Bedrock call fails.

The MCP server remains model-independent:

```text
Bedrock planner -> proposed tool call -> MCPClientBridge -> MCP server
MCP tool handler -> validation + deterministic workflow operation
```

Never place Bedrock calls inside MCP tool handlers.

